### Summary

On x86 builds without AVX-VNNI-INT8, the Q8_0 `vec_dot` returns a sign-flipped product term when the second operand contains -128 at a position where the first operand is negative. The generic C kernel (`ggml_vec_dot_q8_0_q8_0_generic`) gives the exact result.

### Cause

`mul_sum_i8_pairs_float` in `ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/quants.c` (non-`__AVXVNNIINT8__` path):

```c
const __m256i ax = _mm256_sign_epi8(x, x);   // |x|
const __m256i sy = _mm256_sign_epi8(y, x);   // y negated where x < 0
```

Negating int8 -128 overflows back to -128, so that term gets the wrong sign.

### Reproduction (public API only)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include "ggml.h"
#include "ggml-cpu.h"

#define QK 32
typedef struct { uint16_t d; int8_t qs[QK]; } blk;   // layout of block_q8_0

static int check(int8_t x0, int8_t y0) {
    blk x, y;
    memset(&x, 0, sizeof x); memset(&y, 0, sizeof y);
    x.d = y.d = 0x3C00;                                  // fp16 1.0
    x.qs[0] = x0; y.qs[0] = y0;
    float s = 0;
    ggml_get_type_traits_cpu(GGML_TYPE_Q8_0)->vec_dot(QK, &s, 0, &x, 0, &y, 0, 1);
    int exact = x0 * y0;
    printf("x0=%4d y0=%4d  exact=%6d  vec_dot=%8.1f  %s\n", x0, y0, exact, s, s == (float)exact ? "ok" : "WRONG");
    return s != (float)exact;
}

int main(void) {
    ggml_cpu_init();
    int bad = 0;
    bad += check(-1, -127); bad += check(-1, -128); bad += check(1, -128);
    bad += check(-128, 1);  bad += check(-128, -128);
    printf("%d wrong\n", bad);
    return bad != 0;
}
```

