cc repro.c -Iggml/include -Lbuild/bin -lggml-cpu -lggml-base -o repro && LD_LIBRARY_PATH=build/bin ./repro
```

Output at commit 4e416ee, default CMake build (GGML_NATIVE=ON) with clang 18.1.8, on a Zen 4 CPU (AVX-512 VNNI present, `__AVXVNNIINT8__` not defined):

```
x0=  -1 y0=-127  exact=   127  vec_dot=   127.0  ok
x0=  -1 y0=-128  exact=   128  vec_dot=  -128.0  WRONG
x0=   1 y0=-128  exact=  -128  vec_dot=  -128.0  ok
x0=-128 y0=   1  exact=  -128  vec_dot=  -128.0  ok
x0=-128 y0=-128  exact= 16384  vec_dot=-16384.0  WRONG
2 wrong
```

The same two cases also differ between `ggml_vec_dot_q8_0_q8_0` and `ggml_vec_dot_q8_0_q8_0_generic` when `arch/x86/quants.c` is compiled with GCC 13.3 `-mavx2 -mfma -mf16c` or `-march=native`.

### Reach

`quantize_row_q8_0` never produces -128, and in normal inference the second operand is an activation quantised by ggml, so standard llama.cpp inference should not hit this. It affects callers that pass their own int8 data (for example a Q8_0 buffer from another tool), and it makes the SIMD kernel's accepted input range narrower than the generic kernel's. In a larger randomised comparison over the full int8 domain, the AVX2 and generic kernels disagreed on 5,961 of 154,280 blocks, and every disagreeing block contained -128.

Possible resolutions: document (and perhaps assert in debug builds) that Q8_0 values must lie in [-127, 127], or handle -128 in the sign trick. The same helper may be used by other quant types' x86 dot products; I have only checked Q8_0 (the TQ1_0 and TQ2_0 AVX2 kernels agreed with generic on every block in the same comparison).

